**Electric polarization and the internal field** When electric dipoles in a dielectric acquire an orientational bias, or the positive and negative charge distributions within molecules shift relative to one another, macroscopic polarization develops and bound charge can appear;
this process is called electric polarization.
The bound charge here arises from a change in the material's internal charge distribution, not from free charge flowing from one plate through the dielectric to the other.
